From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] net: stmmac: dwmac-sun55i: use the A733's per-channel TX and RX interrupts

The A733's GMAC0 has three interrupt lines, not one (A733 User Manual,
Table 12-1):

  GMAC0_TOP          INTID 204  GIC SPI 172
  GMAC0_PERCH_TX_0   INTID 205  GIC SPI 173
  GMAC0_PERCH_RX_0   INTID 206  GIC SPI 174

The UM names per-channel lines for channel 0 only. The DMA channel count
(MAC_HW_FEATURE2 at 0x04500124, TXCHCNT/RXCHCNT) is still to be read back
on the board: the register description used here places the HW_FEATURE
words one register off against dwmac4.h (GMAC_HW_FEATURE0 = 0x11c). This
patch does not depend on the count. It enables multi-IRQ mode only when
the DT names a line for every queue stmmac uses (one of each by default).

The glue ran stmmac on GMAC0_TOP alone, and stmmac_request_irq_single()
then serves RX and TX completion from one handler on one CPU. With the two
backports before this patch, stmmac_get_platform_resources() reads
"tx-queue-N" and "rx-queue-N" lines from the DT (a31bbe5ca2f8). Their data
is kept only in multi-IRQ mode (dab177cbea34). This patch lets the glue
choose between the two modes:

- stmmac's multi-IRQ mode (STMMAC_FLAG_MULTI_MSI_EN) when the DT names both
  lines for every queue in use;
- the single-IRQ mode otherwise, so a DT with only "macirq" runs exactly as
  before.

In multi-IRQ mode stmmac also sets DMA_Mode.INTM = 1 in dwmac4_dma_init(),
the DMA init of every core from 5.10 up (hwif.c; this one is 0x53).

The selection is the one dwmac-s32 makes upstream, commit 66ccb4f1d205
("stmmac: s32: enable support for Multi-IRQ mode"): the same checks and the
same two log lines. A comment in the code names it.

What this does not change. stmmac_request_irq_multi_msi() hints rx-0 and
tx-0 to cpumask_of(i % num_online_cpus()), which is CPU0 for queue 0, and
re-applies that on every open. That is mainline behaviour -- 6.18.53, and
mainline, net-next and linux-next as of 2026-09-27 -- and it stays. So both
lines start on CPU0, as the single line did. What this buys is RX and TX as
separate interrupts that a user can place apart.

Vendor: the vendor 6.6 kernel requests the same two lines
(eth0:tx-0 on 173, eth0:rx-0 on 174, both at smp_affinity 0) and in
addition SPI 178, GMAC0_PWS_CLK_CTRL, as "eth0:lpi". Nothing here is taken
from its code. 6.18.53's platform code reads no LPI line, so SPI 178 stays
undescribed. Whether it is the LPI interrupt is open.

A523: the new function does not look at the compatible. The binding keeps
the A523's gmac200 at one "macirq", so an A523 stays in single-IRQ mode; the
one visible change there is the "MAC IRQ mode selected" line. That is a code
review item, not tested on an A523.

Open until the board shows it:
- that INTM = 1 routes channel 0's completion to SPI 173 and 174 on this
  integration;
- that those lines are level-high like GMAC0_TOP. The A733 documentation
  available gives no trigger types; the vendor kernel and dwmac-s32's DT use level.

What to observe: end0:tx-0 and end0:rx-0 in /proc/interrupts counting under
one-directional traffic while end0:mac stays near zero, with throughput equal
to single-IRQ mode.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/dwmac-sun55i.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/dwmac-sun55i.c
index 9cc635805..1dea9d116 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/dwmac-sun55i.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/dwmac-sun55i.c
@@ -131,6 +131,48 @@ static int sun55i_gmac200_set_syscon(struct device *dev,
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Per-channel interrupts. The A733's GMAC0 has a line of its own for DMA
+ * channel 0's transmit and for its receive completion, GMAC0_PERCH_TX_0 and
+ * GMAC0_PERCH_RX_0 (SPI 173 and 174, A733 User Manual Table 12-1), next to
+ * GMAC0_TOP (SPI 172, "macirq"). When the DT names a "tx-queue-N" and an
+ * "rx-queue-N" line for every queue in use, run stmmac in its multi-IRQ mode:
+ * RX and TX then get separate handlers and separate interrupts, and stmmac
+ * sets DMA_Mode.INTM = 1 so the core signals completion on those lines. A DT
+ * that names only "macirq" keeps the single-IRQ mode it has always had.
+ *
+ * The selection is the one dwmac-s32 makes upstream, commit 66ccb4f1d205
+ * ("stmmac: s32: enable support for Multi-IRQ mode").
+ */
+static void sun55i_gmac200_setup_multi_irq(struct device *dev,
+					   struct plat_stmmacenet_data *plat,
+					   struct stmmac_resources *res)
+{
+	u32 i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < plat->rx_queues_to_use; i++) {
+		if (res->rx_irq[i] <= 0) {
+			dev_dbg(dev, "no rx-queue-%u interrupt\n", i);
+			goto mac_irq_mode;
+		}
+	}
+
+	for (i = 0; i < plat->tx_queues_to_use; i++) {
+		if (res->tx_irq[i] <= 0) {
+			dev_dbg(dev, "no tx-queue-%u interrupt\n", i);
+			goto mac_irq_mode;
+		}
+	}
+
+	plat->flags |= STMMAC_FLAG_MULTI_MSI_EN;
+	dev_info(dev, "Multi-IRQ mode (per queue IRQs) selected\n");
+	return;
+
+mac_irq_mode:
+	plat->flags &= ~STMMAC_FLAG_MULTI_MSI_EN;
+	dev_info(dev, "MAC IRQ mode selected\n");
+}
+
 static int sun55i_gmac200_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	const struct sun55i_gmac_variant *variant;
@@ -156,6 +198,8 @@ static int sun55i_gmac200_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	plat_dat->flags |= STMMAC_FLAG_SPH_DISABLE;
 	plat_dat->host_dma_width = 32;
 
+	sun55i_gmac200_setup_multi_irq(dev, plat_dat, &stmmac_res);
+
 	/*
 	 * On the A733 the syscon register is GMAC_CFG, the first register of
 	 * the GMAC's own glue block, so it shares the MAC's reset domain: the
